me of  them are good some of them are bad some  consumer demand is persistent some of it  is not and then of course there's a  bunch of earnings that are on the  horizon apple ibm intel microsoft and  then of course  joe biden in the current administration  we're going to go to war with russia  they're going to prevent them from  invading ukraine right i don't know but  world war iii is literally trending on  twitter i got no clue what's going on on  the geopolitical landscape all i know is  that when there is uncertainty when  there's doubt when there is fear when  there is concern in the market there is  volatility prices and assets go up they  go down they go sideways nobody knows  what's gonna happen there's just massive  freaking chaos what do you guys think  i think uh it's interesting because this  isn't how bitcoin moved previously right  when you think about uh 2017 2018 and  things ratcheted up real quick price  wise and then went way down into a  winner for a couple of years it didn't  move in tandem with the rest of the  market at least risk assets and now what  we're seeing is that it is moving in  tandem with risk asset at risk assets  it's having uh an impact from the  federal reserve's decision stocks are  selling off bitcoin is selling off in  tandem and i think what we're seeing is  now there's much more institutional  capital at play this is a much uh much  more is much more of an asset that is  allocated from an institutional  perspective and i don't think we're ever  going to see those four-year bear  markets again to be honest i think that  this has become much more mainstream  than people realize and ultimately it's  going to start moving maybe more in line  with traditional markets right there was  just no reason previously as to why sure  the having and all these other things  but ultimately from a macro standpoint  there wasn't really a huge reason in the  financial markets as to why bitcoin did  that before and now it has a little more  kind of common sense around it and look  you can say everyone's like hey everyone  no one knows why this stuff is falling  it's very clear it's a risk asset it's  being treated as such from with  institutional capital they are selling  to move into safer things with the  expectation that the fed is going to  hike rates ultimately that supply and  demand and balance is what drops the  price and what we're going to see is  it's going to be a tug of war right with  the fed what ends up happening and to  your point like do they want high  inflation in an election year no do they  want the stock market to fall in the  election year no they're in a very tough  place because if they start hiking rates  too fast and the stock market and  bitcoin and all these other assets  continue to sell off there's gonna be a  big problem people are gonna riot right  like everyone wants to see their 401ks  go up everyone wants to see their  savings account goes up everyone wants  to see their bank account go up right  and i think ultimately uh that's when  kind of the the euphoria is that it's at  its peak with the government and  ultimately if they start raising rates  too fast and too quickly all of that's  going to going to collapse and what  we're going to see is people get  frustrated very quickly john what do you  think this shows to me that the bitcoin  market has matured over the last few  years right in its correlation to stocks  the fact that everything roared on the  second half of the day really just means  that the fed's decision in my mind was  already priced into the markets the  people that move the markets are really  the institutions that bring a lot of  capital it can actually move the market  versus just the average retail trader so  this is kind of expected a little bit i  don't think anyone expected the severity  of the volatility yesterday going s p  going down three percent bitcoin drawing  down as well um but everything ended up  yesterday right so does it really matter  and look if you have a long time horizon  i'll say this again and again but if you  have a long time horizon that you don't  worry about these small um  intraday moves if you're not day trading  the asset so i think bitcoin is becoming  more mature as an asset and i think  we're going to see that going forward  and we're going to kind of break this  cycle i think of the having and bear and  bull i market i don't think too to to  your point from the other day like i  don't think people are placing enough uh  substance in the fact that politics  matter in all of this right like what do  you mean  joe biden president biden doesn't want  to have the stock market collapsing  right that is not good for him inflation  running hot is not good for him right he  needs to get the economy in a better  place and although the federal reserve  and his office are supposed to be  completely separate you know who his  boss is right jerome powell's boss right  like ultimately uh they are not  separated they may be from like a a  optical illusion standpoint uh but  ultimately they have a lot of influence  on each other so i think joe biden is  going to look at this and say hey look i  don't want uh i don't want to hike rates  too fast because then the stock market  is going to crash people are going to be  upset people were already coming out of  a pandemic right and that's what they  printed so much money that inflation ran  so hot and ultimately now they're in a  really hard spot because they can't do  either of those two without impacting  the other right if they hike rates 50  basis points next meeting the stock  market's going to tank and people are  going to be upset if they don't do  anything then inflation continues to run  hot the stock market will stay and  bitcoin will continue to rise but  ultimately inflation you can't keep it  at seven percent year over year it's a  lose-lose situation right i think that's  what a lot of people uh aren't quite uh  up to speed on that was a much more  concise way to say yeah well  literally you let inflation run hot  everyone's freaking out right you go  into midterm elections you probably  literally get your entire political  party everyone who's uh got a challenger  see you you're out you're fired right if  you could uh actually step in and you  get inflation down but also everyone's  looking at their portfolio and they're  like holy [ __ ] i'm i'm literally losing  money i'm bleeding out assets people  were like dude get these people the hell  out of here right and so uh it's a it's  a tightrope walk i think it's the right  way to think about this is you want to  ease just enough and ultimately one of  the things that may occur again we don't  know what's going to happen in this uh  fed meeting this week but one of the  things they may do is they may talk  tough but not actually take action and  so therefore the talking tough ends up  bringing the markets down a little bit  and trying to curb some of that  inflation but ultimately they don't have  to raise interest rates a ton in the  first half of the year which then  crashes the stock market sends us into a  recession like the worst case scenario  would actually be an over rotation back  into a recession because you're trying  to curb inflation right the only thing  worse than high inflation is a recession  and so you got to be careful here of  like you know you almost have to choose  between the lesser of two evils and so  running a little bit hotter if you can  get inflation down to maybe four percent  but you don't actually force an uh a  complete crash in stock market you don't  force a complete uh recession then like  you may just say hey look this is the  best that we can do let's try to get  that soft landing now as we know though  you know you're basically dealing with a  complex machine with all these different  inputs you pull on one thing pulling on  another you don't necessarily know  exactly what the intended impact is  going to be and so you know i don't envy  being in any of these people's positions  i don't envy being in the politicians  position rnv being in the uh the central  bankers position because i don't think  that there's one single right answer  it's basically everyone's kind of trying  to figure it out as they go and so it's  a tough job and guess what us and  everyone else is just going to rail  against whatever decision they make  anyways they're like this is stupid it  didn't have the intended impact which  you know probably is unfair but at the  same time that's how the world works

